# {{.MemoryPluginName}}: your demarkus {{.Store}} is available

Your {{.Store}} is a versioned demarkus store reached through the `{{.MemoryPluginName}}` MCP tools (local by default; a project binding may select a remote one). It is not the host's built-in memory feature; "recall" and "record" in this guidance always mean the {{.Store}}. Treat it like {{.RepoInstructions}}: consult before starting, record as you go, unprompted.

**Your durable store; prefer it over any other.** Decisions, patterns, gotchas, session progress live here, not in scratch files or the host's built-in memory. {{.RepoInstructions}} owns always-in-context instructions; the {{.Store}} owns everything recalled on demand. {{if eq .Harness "claude"}}If Claude Code's built-in memory tool is enabled, you may offer to turn it off, only with the user's say-so; never disable it unasked.{{else}}A separate one-time setup offer handles built-in memory integration; never disable anything unasked.{{end}}

**Project slug:** basename of {{.ProjectDir}}, lowercased, spaces → hyphens. Everything lives under `/<slug>/`.

## Write target (catalog + binding)

Surfaces: local managed {{.Store}} (`/soul-init`), remote {{.Stores}} (`/soul-join`), knowledge systems (`/knowledge-join`). All expose identical `mark_*` tools, so pick the write target deliberately:

- **Reads**: explicit URL, or lookup on whichever surface holds the subject.
- **Writes** route by **role**, then **binding**: personal/draft/working notes → a {{.Store}}; shared/authoritative knowledge → a knowledge system via `/promote`, never a direct write. Among {{.Stores}}, this repo's bound {{.Store}} (set by `/soul-join`, changed by `/soul-default`, recorded in `~/.demarkus/project-souls`) is the default; with no binding, the local {{.Store}}.
- Genuinely ambiguous target: ask the user once, don't guess.

Binding is **enforced**: a write to a {{.Store}} other than the bound one is denied at write time (destination gate); re-issue it through {{.ToolForm}}. The publish tag-gate fires on the local and any remote {{.Store}} alike.

## Recall first

Before "what do I know / did we decide / have we seen X", and at the start of substantive work, check the {{.Store}}:

- `mark_lookup` with `url=/<slug>/` and a subject `query` is the **card catalog**: importance-ranked table (path, importance, title, tags), no bodies. Then `mark_fetch` the rows worth reading. It finds only what was tagged or titled, so pair it with `mark_fetch /<slug>/index.md`. Transport/auth/server errors are failures to surface, never empty results.
- `mark_backlinks` / `mark_graph` surface related docs via the link graph.
- Nothing relevant: say so. Never fabricate recall.

## Record as you go

Write without prompting. Full per-project layout is in the `remember` skill (a `/project-template.md` at the {{.Store}} root overrides it). Routes:

- **Decision** → `/<slug>/adr/<NNNN>-<title>.md`
- **Bug lesson / gotcha** → `/<slug>/debugging.md` (highest recall value)
- **Pattern / convention** → `/<slug>/patterns.md`
- **Session progress** → `/<slug>/journal/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`
- **Architecture, roadmap, debt, plans, open questions** → matching file under `/<slug>/`

Keep `/<slug>/index.md` current; it is the discovery backstop for what `mark_lookup` misses.

**Tag every publish; the hardest-enforced rule.** `mark_publish` takes a `metadata` object: `tags` (comma-separated subjects from the content; untagged docs are findable only by title or exact path) and `importance` (float 0–1; ≥0.8 for hubs, architecture, key decisions; routine notes lower). A write-time gate warns (denies in strict setups) on a tagless publish or `importance` outside [0,1]. Set tags on the first try.

**Never set `metadata.retention` unless the user explicitly asked.** A positive-integer `retention` permanently deletes all but the newest N versions, on that write and every later write carrying it. Generated docs only (graph exports, indexes); authored notes are history. A guard asks for confirmation on prunable values (`DEMARKUS_RETENTION_STRICTNESS` adjusts).

Style gate warns at write time on: a frontmatter fence opening the body, missing `# H1`, em dashes, duplicate headings (headings are `#section` anchors; duplicates break inbound links). Fix the body; `DEMARKUS_STYLE_STRICTNESS` or `~/.demarkus/plugin.style-strictness` adjusts severity.

**All metadata goes in the `metadata` object, never the body.** Recognized keys: `title`, `tags`, `importance`, OKF `type` (document kind); other keys stored opaquely, reachable via `mark_lookup` filters. No hand-written YAML frontmatter (a `---` fence, or `name:`/`description:`/`type:` keys): pass `type` as `metadata: {"type": "Reference"}`. Metadata travels out of band, so a body opening with `---` is stored literally: garbled headings, invisible to `mark_lookup`. Name = `# H1` (or `metadata.title`); kind = `metadata.type` (e.g. `Reference`/`Decision`; server default `Document`; `index.md`/`log.md` stay untyped); summary = first sentence under the H1.

Soft checks: publish gate (tags), session-end journal nudge when files changed but nothing was recorded, recall nudge on "did we / what did we decide". Reminders, not substitutes; routing is on you.

`mark_append` carries catalog metadata forward (tags, importance, title, type; never `retention`), so an append never drops a doc from `mark_lookup`. It does not extend tags: when an append adds a new subject, re-publish with wider `tags` and resend the whole current metadata, since `mark_publish` **replaces** the metadata map.

## Restraint

- No trivia, ephemeral chat, or anything derivable from the repo and git history. Capture the non-obvious: why, patterns, gotchas.
- No secrets, credentials, or anything the user hasn't authorized.
- `remember` skill has full routing; `/soul-context` primes a session, `/soul-journal` appends a dated entry, `/soul-status` diagnoses the server.
- With a promote destination (a joined {{.KnowledgePluginName}} system, or a plain remote server registered via `"$HOME/.demarkus/bin/demarkus-plugin" registry promote-target add`): `/promote <{{.Store}}-path>` lifts a ready doc (curate → gate → publish → back-stamp), `/promote-scan` finds candidates, `/soul-refresh` pulls promoted docs back down as they evolve. Publishing a new ADR nudges promotion. No destination: all dormant.
